  "result": "**Root cause:** `parseAmount` in `src/money.js` only accepted plain digits like `12.50`. Prices with a thousands separator, such as `\"2,450.50\"` or `\"1,299.00\"`, didn't match its regex. The match returned `null`, and the next line threw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')`, which surfaced as a 500 on `POST /checkout`. The log shows 37 failing payloads, and every one has a price of the form `9,999.99`. That is why the errors looked intermittent: they only hit orders containing a price of 1,000 or more.\n\n**Fix (`src/money.js`):**\n- The parser now accepts properly grouped thousands separators (`1,299.00`) and strips the commas before converting.\n- Malformed input like `abc` or `1,23.00` now throws the documented `Invalid amount: <input>` error instead of a `TypeError`.\n\n**Tests (`test/money.test.js`):** I added three tests:\n- Thousands-separator parsing.\n- Rejection of invalid amounts.\n- An end-to-end `checkout` with a `\"1,299.00\"` price.\n\n`npm test` passes with 7 of 7 tests. I didn't run the new tests against the old code, but the regression cases use the same price format the log shows failing.\n\nI didn't commit anything.\n\n**Still open:** `parseAmount` now throws a clear `Invalid amount` error for bad input, but I didn't check whether `src/server.js` turns that into a 400 or lets it become a 500.",
